{
  "image_name": "0e29bb402fd33e470385be5b7a353192.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure contrasts two sides of the immune landscape in hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC): immune cells that attack tumor cells and immune populations that suppress that response. In the center is a cluster of HCC cells, with the left side showing antitumor effectors engaging them through receptor–ligand interactions and antigen recognition. Dendritic cells appear to prime T cells, leading to immunogenic activation of CD8+ tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes and tissue-resident memory T cells that recognize tumor antigens via TCR–MHC interactions. CD4+ T cells seem to support this response through cytokines such as IL-2 and IFNγ. Innate-like lymphocytes, including γδ T cells and NK cells, also target the tumor through pathways involving NKG2D, butyrophilin, and TRAIL.\n\nThe right side summarizes immune regulators that limit antitumor immunity. Several suppressive myeloid and lymphoid populations are shown, including tolerogenic dendritic cells, neutrophils, granulocytic and monocytic MDSCs, TAM/Kupffer cells, regulatory T cells, and regulatory B cells. These cells express inhibitory molecules such as PD-L1, CTLA4, and PD-1 or release suppressive mediators including IL-10, TGFβ, IDO, arginase, and VEGF. Metabolic suppression, like glucose and glutamine depletion, and inflammatory TNF-associated conditions in NASH are also highlighted as factors that weaken effective immune control of HCC.",
